* Storage-key write protection at PAGE granularity -- CP-67's other half.
*
* 09-frame-sharing.rc proved the first half: two address spaces can share a
* frame at 4 KB granularity inside a 1 MB segment, so segment size does not
* dictate sharing granularity.  That gives SHARING.  It does not give
* READ-ONLY sharing, and CP-67 needed both:
*
*     For store protection of the shared pages, the users are run with
*     protection key = F.  All shared pages' storage keys are set to zero
*     and all other pages belonging to these users have storage keys = F.
*         -- CP-67 Program Logic Manual, GY20-0590-2, p.114
*
* and PAGTRANS: "If shared page, get key '0', non-shared pages get key 'F'."
*
* The two mechanisms are INDEPENDENT.  The page tables decide WHAT is shared;
* the storage keys decide whether a sharer may WRITE it.  Neither involves the
* segment size, which is the whole point of docs/05-CP67-PRIOR-ART.md.
*
* THE SHAPE.  One address space, two frames, both above the line:
*
*     virtual 01005000 -> real 01100000   storage key 0   "shared"
*     virtual 01006000 -> real 01200000   storage key F   private
*
* Then SPKA to PSW key F and try three things:
*
*     store to the private page    must SUCCEED   key F PSW vs key F storage
*     fetch from the shared page   must SUCCEED   no fetch protection set
*     store to the shared page     must FAIL      key F PSW vs key 0 storage
*
* The third is the assertion.  A store is permitted when the PSW key is zero
* OR matches the storage key; F against 0 is neither, so it must take a
* protection exception -- program interruption code 4.
*
* TWO THINGS THE TEST DOES THAT ARE WORTH COPYING
* -----------------------------------------------
* It reads the key back with ISKE before relying on it.  A test that merely
* assumed SSKE worked would pass for the wrong reason if the key were never
* set, because an unset key is 0 -- the value being tested for.
*
* And it carries the "which store is in flight" marker in R10, a REGISTER.
* Registers survive an interruption, and after SPKA almost no frame is
* writable -- which is the situation under test, so storage cannot hold the
* marker.  That lets the handler tell "the shared store faulted, as intended"
* from "the private store faulted, a real failure".
*
* NEGATIVE CONTROL -- RUN, AND IT FIRES
* -------------------------------------
*     r 2024=417000F0     give the shared frame key F as well
*     r 203E=4700         disable the ISKE guard, which would otherwise catch it
*                         -> 001203, "the store was allowed"
*
* Append both lines after the pokes to reproduce.  The first attempt at this
* control patched the ISKE to read the other frame, which broke the guard
* rather than bypassing it and reported 001201 -- a reminder that a control
* has to be checked as carefully as the test.
*
* HOW TO READ THE RESULT
*   00600C   pass.  Page-granular read-only sharing works on ESA/390.
*   001201   the key never got set -- ISKE read it back non-zero.  SSKE
*            problem, not a protection problem.
*   001202   a FETCH from the key 0 frame failed.  The fetch-protection bit
*            got set somehow; this test deliberately leaves it off.
*   001203   THE STORE WAS ALLOWED.  No protection at all -- see the control.
*   001204   something faulted BEFORE the assertion, R10 still 1.  The store
*            to the private key F frame was rejected, which should not happen.
*   001205   it faulted, but not with code 4.  Read X'8E' for the real code.
*
* ENCODINGS, both cross-checked against z390's opcode table:
*   SSKE  B22B  RRE   r1 holds the key, r2 the real frame address
*   ISKE  B229  RRE
*   SPKA  B20A  S     takes the new key from bits 24-27 of the operand
*                     address, so SPKA 240(0) sets key F
*
* KEY ADDRESSES
*   002024  LA 7,0     the shared frame's key -- patch to 417000F0 for the control
*   00203E  BNZ        the ISKE guard -- patch to 4700 to disable
*   002076  PGMH       the handler, reached via the program new PSW at X'68'
*
* Built by build/key1.py.
*
r 0=0008000000002000
